  "body": "The spec has the following rules in the rendering section\n\n```\nol[type=a], li[type=a] { list-style-type: lower-alpha; }\nol[type=A], li[type=A] { list-style-type: upper-alpha; }\nol[type=i], li[type=i] { list-style-type: lower-roman; }\nol[type=I], li[type=I] { list-style-type: upper-roman; }\n```\n\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#lists\n\nand the following requirement about matching selectors\n\n> Attribute selectors on an HTML element in an HTML document must treat the values of attributes with the following names as ASCII case-insensitive, with one exception as noted below:\n> - type (except as specified below)\n> \n> The exception to the list above is the type attribute on ol elements, which must be treated as case-sensitive.\n\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#selectors\n\nTwo problems here:\n- The UA stylesheet, if it follows the requirements about matching selectors, doesn't work correctly for the `li` element.\n- WebKit/Blink/Gecko/IE11 are case-insensitive for both ol[type=a] and li[type=a] in author stylesheet.\n  http://software.hixie.ch/utilities/js/live-dom-viewer/saved/3660\n\nPossible solutions:\n- Add `li` as an exception to be case-sensitive.\n- Remove `ol` as an exception (matching browsers) and either say that both `ol` and `li` are exceptions for the UA stylesheet, or make all attribute values be case-sensitive in the UA stylesheet (and use the `i` flag to opt in to case-insensitive).\n",
